course: dictionary
lesson: 1640
title: Childishly: acting younger than your age
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Childishly comes from child. It describes how someone acts, and it usually means acting younger than their age. «Grown-ups sometimes behave childishly; you can love them and still see they got it wrong.» The grown-ups are acting like small children, and the writer thinks they made a mistake.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. Sulking, stamping and throwing a tantrum are childish when a grown-up or an older child does them. The word is usually a criticism, so it tells you the behaviour is not right for that person's age.
explain: Sometimes the word is kind. Childishly happy means simple, innocent joy, like a child who is delighted by a balloon. Ask yourself: is the writer cross or fond? That tells you which meaning is being used.
fact: Childishly is an adverb, so it tells you how someone does something.
fact: «Childishly» means in a silly, immature way that is not right for someone's age.
fact: «Childishly» can also mean simply and innocently, as in «childishly happy».
fact: A similar word is immaturely. The opposite is maturely.
exercise: «Grown-ups sometimes behave childishly.» What does childishly mean here? | In a wise, grown-up way | Quietly and politely | *In a silly, immature way that is not right for their age | Very quickly
exercise: Which word is the opposite of childishly? | Immaturely | *Maturely | Silly | Happily
exercise: Which sentence uses childishly in its kind meaning? | *She was childishly happy with her new balloon, bouncing on the grass | He childishly refused to say sorry | The old man stamped his feet and childishly threw his hat | He childishly shouted at the referee
test: Ruby says her big brother is acting childishly when he sulks and slams doors. What does the word tell you about him? | He is very tired and needs a nap | He is acting younger than his age, and it is a criticism | *He is acting younger than his age, and the writer is not pleased | He is wiser than most adults
test: Which of these words is closest in meaning to childishly when it is a criticism? | Bravely | Calmly | Gracefully | *Immaturely
exam: Which meaning fits best in «Grown-ups sometimes behave childishly; you can love them and still see they got it wrong.»? | *In a silly, immature way for their age | In a brave way that shows courage | In a grown-up, sensible way | In a simple, innocent way that makes everyone smile
